"""The access list: one checked-in entry per HTTP route and MCP tool.

## Principles

- Regular (non-admin) users change nothing platform-managed except their own
  private agents and their own personal state (profile, MFA, sessions, chat,
  home, notifications, memory, own OAuth/MCP connections).
- Global entities and `_repo` (the git-synced workspace) are Platform-
  boundary only: platform admins, and running workflows through workflow
  permissions. Today the provider-org scope bypass (see
  `api/src/repositories/README.md`) also reaches some of them; those entries
  carry `intended_change` and lose that path at the R3 cutover. Never add a
  new route that relies on provider-org membership for access.
- MCP matches REST exactly: an MCP tool with a REST binding in the operation
  catalog (`src.services.operation_catalog.OPERATION_CATALOG`) inherits that
  route's access class, permission, boundary, and current gate verbatim.
- Never grant a write permission to the User base role. Pick the narrowest
  class available (`personal` / `execute` / `own_private_agent` /
  `table_policy` before falling back to `permission`). If a route's correct
  class is genuinely unclear, leave a `# QUESTION:` comment next to it and
  report it — never guess.

## How to add an entry for a new route

1. Add the route to its router as usual; nothing here changes request
   handling.
2. Add one `AccessEntry` below (kept sorted by path/method, MCP tools last
   sorted by tool name). Set `current_gate` to what the route's DEPENDENCY
   TREE mechanically enforces today (see `CurrentGate`) — never to what an
   inline check narrows it to; if the handler or a service it calls one
   level down does its own is_superuser/is_platform_admin/has_scope_bypass/
   is_provider_org/mcp_write_scope_bypass check beyond the dependency,
   record those tokens in `inline_checks` instead, and set `inline_effect`
   (`InlineEffect`) to what that check does to the caller — read the handler
   and the service it calls; a token can sit in a claim or a repository
   argument without gating anyone (`no_caller_effect`). A check the one-hop
   token scan cannot see (a module-local helper, a deeper service) still gets
   its `inline_effect`, with `reason` saying where the check lives. Set
   `access_class` to the
   narrowest class the classification rules support (in short:
   public/personal/execute/own_private_agent/table_policy/embed before
   permission).
3. If `access_class` is `permission`, set `permission` (`<domain>.<read|
   readwrite|execute>[.all]`) and `boundary`. Prefer the operation catalog's
   `action_scopes` when the route is catalogued (`operation_id` set). Use
   `.all` for extended management detail on objects the caller can already
   reach (an app's source, which roles share a form), never for reaching
   more organizations. A route that returns a decrypted secret is
   `secrets.read`.
4. If `current_gate` is `engine_or_bypass`, or `inline_checks` contains
   `has_scope_bypass` or `mcp_write_scope_bypass`, set `intended_change`
   (default: "Provider-org non-admins lose this access at the R3 cutover;
   platform admins keep it.") unless `reason` already states why permanent
   provider-org access is intended.
5. Run `./test.sh tests/unit/test_access_list.py -v` — it fails loudly if
   the entry is missing, duplicated, points at a route that no longer
   exists, disagrees with the route's actual dependency gate, or is missing
   a required `intended_change`.
6. Regenerate the JSON projection: `python api/scripts/generate_access_list.py`
   (or let the freshness test tell you it's stale).

## Generation note

This file's entries were bootstrapped by walking the live FastAPI app's
routes (`src.main.app.routes`) and the registered MCP tools
(`src.services.mcp_server.server.get_system_tools`); mechanically deriving
`current_gate` from each route's dependency-tree identity (matching the four
canonical auth-dependency callables, never string names) and `inline_checks`
from the handler's source plus, up to one hop, any function or non-canonical
dependency callable it calls that lives in `shared.`/`src.` (excluding the
canonical auth resolvers themselves and the JWT-minting helpers, whose own
source embeds principal fields as claims, not as checks); and hand-
classifying `access_class` per the principles above (domain-by-domain review
of every non-superuser/non-public route, cross-checked against the specific
authorization helper each route/service calls). Catalogued routes and their
bound MCP tools inherit `action_scopes` from `OPERATION_CATALOG` directly;
entries generated through catalog-bound MCP tools carry a reason noting the
inheritance, and MCP transport routes always show `current_gate=
authenticated` (a valid OAuth session is the transport-level floor) with any
further tool-specific restriction captured in `inline_checks`.
"""

